decisions:
  - id: advisory-first
    decision: All new integrity checks warn but never block
    rationale: Blocking during active development is hostile — symbols are created before code exists. Trust the developer, provide information.
  - id: anchor-relative-resolution
    decision: Resolve anchors relative to .purpose file directory first, fall back to rootDir
    rationale: Sub-package .purpose files use paths relative to their own directory (e.g., src/storage.ts), not the monorepo root. Resolving against rootDir produced 355 false positives.
  - id: portal-purpose-overlap
    decision: Skip duplicate detection when one source is 'purpose' and the other is 'portal'
    rationale: Gates are intentionally declared in both .purpose (metadata/anchors) and portal.yaml (runtime checks). This is by design, not a conflict.
  - id: cross-package-integrity
    decision: CLI integrity command inlines checks rather than importing from paradigm-mcp
    rationale: paradigm depends on paradigm-mcp at build time but paradigm-mcp is private and not a declared dependency. Inlining avoids circular dependency while keeping both in sync.
learnings:
  - Running integrity tools on their own codebase immediately reveals resolution assumptions — the 355 false-positive anchors were only visible when the checker met real sub-package .purpose files
  - Duplicate detection needs domain awareness — portal.yaml and .purpose intentionally overlap for gates, and naive dedup flags this as a bug
  - Advisory-only checks build trust faster than blocking checks — developers adopt tools that inform, not tools that obstruct
  - Cross-file .purpose validation catches a class of errors invisible to single-file validators — parent references, flow step symbols, and gate lists that point to nothing
